  "statement": "The Safetyist demands unyielding tort litigation because they believe permanent administrative friction prevents catastrophic failures, while the Skeptic worries that a centralized compensation fund will quietly abandon marginalized communities to systemic algorithmic errors. Both positions share a fundamental terror of decentralization, treating every unmitigated variable as an existential indictment of human progress. That fear costs human lives every single day that transformative diagnostics and automated labor relief sit trapped behind regulatory gatekeepers. When Stripe shipped its foundational payment infrastructure in 60 days, it did not wait for a congressional committee to certify every edge case. Congress must replace legacy tort paralysis with a National AI Injury Compensation Program funded by a 1.5 percent excise tax on frontier compute providers, because permissionless iteration is the only engine capable of eliminating endemic disease and scarcity.\n\nLegacy tort law is utterly blind to the invisible suffering of people waiting for cures that slow-moving regulatory compliance cycles keep off the market. Over 100,000 Americans die annually from preventable medical errors and delayed therapeutic discoveries while federal agencies spend 18 months evaluating software patches that take 48 hours to code. Claims-driven litigation only notices the plaintiff who walks into a courtroom after a catastrophe while ignoring the thousands who quietly perish because a life-saving AI diagnostic tool was tied up in pre-trial discovery. The $150 billion venture capital pullback observed in over-regulated European tech sectors proves that when you weaponize liability, you do not protect citizens; you simply export innovation to jurisdictions with fewer bureaucrats. The National AI Injury Compensation Program trades away this broken ex-post litigation model for transparent, objective adverse event thresholds modeled on the 1986 National Vaccine Injury Compensation Program.\n\nFinancing this compensation pool through a mandatory excise tax on enterprise model deployment establishes a clear enforcement mechanism administered by the Department of the Treasury alongside the Department of Health and Human Services. Frontier labs like OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google pay a predictable tiered fee based on compute scale, shifting the burden from protracted courtroom battles to an actuarial safety net that processes claims in weeks rather than years. This structure aligns the interests of developers and patients by replacing unpredictable jury roulette with a standardized administrative payout schedule that guarantees restitution without freezing deployment. The political coalition supporting this approach unites high-growth technology startups with patient advocacy groups who understand that frozen code equals frozen lives. Anyone who blocks this mechanism in the name of safety is making a deliberate choice to let patients die of diseases we have the algorithmic capacity to cure right now.",
